UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 38, Lesson 1: Healthy habits at home', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well. At home they include eating a variety of foods, drinking water, being active, sleeping well, washing hands, limiting screen time and spending time with family. A habit is built by repeating a small action at the same time or place, with a cue (a reminder) and a reward. Small steps work better than big changes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPick a habit you would like to build, then write a cue, an action and a reward.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner. Action: two minutes of brushing. Reward: a sticker on a chart. A good habit plan has a clear cue and a small action that is easy to repeat.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a habit forms in one day. Habits are built by repeating small actions over time.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose two healthy habits to build for two weeks. Write a cue, an action and a reward for each, and track them on a chart. After two weeks, write what helped, what was hard and one change.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well. At home they include eating a variety of foods, drinking water, being active, sleeping well, washing hands, limiting screen time and spending time with family. A habit is built by repeating a small action at the same time or place, with a cue (a reminder) and a reward. Small steps work better than big changes.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose two healthy habits to build for two weeks. Write a cue, an action and a reward for each, and track them on a chart. After two weeks, write what helped, what was hard and one change. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a healthy habit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking a habit forms in one day.. Correction: Sample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a habit forms in one day. Habits are built by repeating small actions over time. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a healthy habit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a healthy habit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a healthy habit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a healthy habit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a healthy habit. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner","Thinking a habit forms in one day","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Pick a habit you would like to build, then write a cue, an action and a reward. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a healthy habit; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner","Careful use of a cue and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking a habit forms in one day","Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a healthy habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a cue and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a cue and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a healthy habit","Apply a healthy habit to new material and name the a cue and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking a habit forms in one day"],"question":"Which response best applies a healthy habit with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on a healthy habit. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking a habit forms in one day"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for a healthy habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking a habit forms in one day","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with a healthy habit","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a healthy habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school","Thinking a habit forms in one day"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a healthy habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a healthy habit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a cue and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Choose two healthy habits to build for two weeks","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking a habit forms in one day"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a healthy habit to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a healthy habit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a healthy habit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a healthy habit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a healthy habit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a healthy habit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking a habit forms in one day.. Correction: Sample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w38-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a healthy habit.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a healthy habit with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a cue and explain why it matters.","I can practise an action independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a reward needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a healthy habit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school. Cue: he puts his water bottle next to his school bag. Action: drink a glass when he gets home. Reward: a tick on a chart. After two weeks he has ticked 12 of 14 days. He then adds a second habit, stretching before bed.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPick a habit you would like to build, then write a cue, an action and a reward.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner. Action: two minutes of brushing. Reward: a sticker on a chart. A good habit plan has a clear cue and a small action that is easy to repeat.","explain":"Healthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well. At home they include eating a variety of foods, drinking water, being active, sleeping well, washing hands, limiting screen time and spending time with family. A habit is built by repeating a small action at the same time or place, with a cue (a reminder) and a reward. Small steps work better than big changes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a habit forms in one day. Habits are built by repeating small actions over time. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A habit cue and action.","caption":"A cue starts a habit.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bottle by the school bag</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Action</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">drink a glass</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school. Cue: he puts his water bottle next to his school bag. Action Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school. Cue: he puts his water bottle next to his school bag. Action Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a healthy habit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school. Cue: he puts his water bottle next to his school bag. Action: drink a glass when he gets home. Reward: a tick on a chart. After two weeks he has ticked 12 of 14 days. He then adds a second habit, stretching before bed. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"A reward and progress.","caption":"Track your habit.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Reward</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tick on a chart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Progress</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">12 of 14 days</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a healthy habit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a habit forms in one day. Habits are built by repeating small actions over time. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school. Cue: he puts his water bottle next to his school bag. Action: drink a glass when he gets home. Reward: a tick on a chart. After two weeks he has ticked 12 of 14 days. He then adds a second habit, stretching before bed.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: Cue: brush teeth after dinner. Action: two minutes of brushing. Reward: a sticker on a chart. A good habit plan has a clear cue and a small action that is easy to repeat.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking a habit forms in one day. Habits are built by repeating small actions over time. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a healthy habit with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nHealthy habits are things we do regularly that keep our bodies and minds well. At home they include eating a variety of foods, drinking water, being active, sleeping well, washing hands, limiting screen time and spending time with family. A habit is built by repeating a small action at the same time or place, with a cue (a reminder) and a reward. Small steps work better than big changes. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 38, Lesson 1: Healthy habits at home","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":38,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Tom builds a habit of drinking water after school. Cue: he puts his water bottle next to his school bag. Action: drink a glass when he gets home. Reward: a tick on a chart. After two weeks he has ticked 12 of 14 days. He then adds a second habit, stretching before bed.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A habit cue 